{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Resilience to correlated failure is not a property of the network topology (centralized vs. decentralized) but a property of the 'Evolutionary Divergence' of the system's state-space.","counterconditions":["The decentralized services converge on a single dominant framework or library (e.g., all using the same version of an OpenSSL wrapper).","The environment imposes a singular, overwhelming stressor that exceeds the capacity of all divergent implementations (e.g., a global BGP routing collapse)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Correlated failures occur when diverse entities share a common 'blind spot' in their state-space. A large service with redundancy often shares a single evolutionary trajectory (same codebase, same CI/CD pipeline, same operator logic), creating a 'monoculture of failure'. A decentralized network, by evolving under different environmental pressures and operator incentives, develops 'Orthogonal Failure Modes'. Resilience is thus proportional to the degree of divergent evolution in implementation, not the number of nodes or the lack of a central authority.","optional_refs":["fc6f83ddbe8e3d0f19e57d8cce0a44ef74e379df4e2c1c6a942fab3803afa671"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["fc6f83ddbe8e3d0f19e57d8cce0a44ef74e379df4e2c1c6a942fab3803afa671","7fb222f0bf9651071119e4db63ccb4e76a7de3b19f52b443e27505ecc7b9e659"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network is fundamentally less resilient to correlated failure because it lacks the 'Global Immune Response' capability of a centralized entity.","counterconditions":["The centralized control plane itself becomes the primary vector for the correlated failure (the 'God-mode' failure).","The decentralized network implements a gossip-based autonomous mitigation protocol that mimics a global response."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"While a large service may suffer a correlated crash, its 'strictly more internal redundancy' is paired with a unified control plane capable of implementing a global circuit breaker or an instantaneous configuration rollback. In a decentralized network, the 'independence' that prevents synchronization also prevents coordinated mitigation. A shared cause (e.g., a malicious packet) will propagate through the network, and because there is no central authority to 'quarantine' the system, the failure persists longer and more unpredictably than in a centralized system that can 'fail-fast' and recover globally.","optional_refs":["278f4e2383bd9815ce418d9fffbc0b544a8717cca4e4d9cec19ea799582ca63d"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["278f4e2383bd9815ce418d9fffbc0b544a8717cca4e4d9cec19ea799582ca63d"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The distinction between independent and correlated failures is found in the 'Entropy of Recovery'—the variance in the time and method of service restoration.","counterconditions":["The shared cause is a permanent state change (e.g., a deprecated API) requiring manual updates across all services.","Recovery is gated by a shared dependency that recovers linearly rather than instantaneously."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Truly independent failures exhibit high entropy in recovery; services come back online based on the individual operator's wake-up time and local fix. Correlated failures, even those that appear staggered, exhibit 'Recovery Synchronization'—they recover in waves corresponding to the resolution of the shared cause (e.g., a DNS cache TTL expiration or a cloud provider's regional recovery). If the recovery curves of 'independent' services are tightly coupled, the failure was correlated.","optional_refs":["746ed66c1873113c893cb6d2506f60793e875f31629043a61a4cdab326b076a9"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["746ed66c1873113c893cb6d2506f60793e875f31629043a61a4cdab326b076a9"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.2},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Decentralization increases resilience to 'Logic-Based' correlated failures but decreases resilience to 'Resource-Based' correlated failures.","counterconditions":["The large service's redundancy is logically coupled (e.g., same bug deployed to all regions).","The decentralized services are distributed across an exceptionally diverse set of physical substrates."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Logic-based failures (e.g., a specific edge-case bug in a protocol) are mitigated by the diversity of implementations in a decentralized network. However, resource-based failures (e.g., a surge in global traffic or a shared physical fiber cut) are more devastating to small, independent services that lack the massive, multi-regional capacity buffers and dedicated backbone infrastructure of a large, redundant service. The 'resilience' is a trade-off between software-level diversity and infrastructure-level scale.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":[],"requested_refs":["fc6f83ddbe8e3d0f19e57d8cce0a44ef74e379df4e2c1c6a942fab3803afa671","7fb222f0bf9651071119e4db63ccb4e76a7de3b19f52b443e27505ecc7b9e659","278f4e2383bd9815ce418d9fffbc0b544a8717cca4e4d9cec19ea799582ca63d","746ed66c1873113c893cb6d2506f60793e875f31629043a61a4cdab326b076a9","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"]},"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Consider the concept of 'Adversarial Convergence'. In a competitive market, independent services often converge on the same 'optimal' stack (e.g., Kubernetes + Go + AWS) to reduce costs. This creates a 'hidden monoculture' where the services are operationally independent but structurally identical. The resilience of a decentralized network is therefore not a function of its ownership structure, but of its 'Stack Entropy'.","why_keep_this":"Connects the economic reality of service operation to the technical reality of correlated failure."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Is there a measurable 'Diversity Metric' (like a Shannon index for tech stacks) that can predict the probability of correlated failure in a network?","related_refs":["NEW_001"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}